<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Académie française</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1635">1635</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Académie française is the French institution responsible for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-language">French language</a>, founded in 1635 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardinal-richelieu">Cardinal Richelieu</a> under Louis XIII. Its forty members, known as the immortals, prepare the official dictionary of the language and rule on its usage. Suppressed during the Revolution in 1793, it was restored under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> and remains the most prestigious of the academies of the Institut de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/academie-francaise.jpg" alt="Académie française" width="320" height="533" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dennis G. Jarvis · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:French_Institute,_Paris_2014_002.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Académie grew from an informal literary group that came out of the salons held at the Hôtel de Rambouillet in the late 1620s and early 1630s. It met at the house of Valentin Conrart and then had nine members. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardinal-richelieu">Cardinal Richelieu</a> made himself its protector, and new members were appointed in 1634. On 22 February 1635, at Richelieu's urging, King Louis XIII granted letters patent establishing it; those registered at the Parlement de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 10 July 1637 charged it to give exact rules to the language and make it capable of treating the arts and sciences. Richelieu's model was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accademia-della-crusca">Accademia della Crusca</a>, founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> in 1582.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-convention">National Convention</a> suppressed all royal academies: in 1792 the election of replacements for dead members was prohibited, and in 1793 the academies were abolished. In 1795 a single body, the Institut de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, replaced them. Napoleon Bonaparte, as First Consul, restored the academies in 1803 only as divisions of the Institut. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xviii">Louis XVIII</a> came to the throne in 1816 each class regained the title Académie, and since then its existence has been uninterrupted. The head of state has been the Académie's protector since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> took the role in 1672, after Richelieu and Pierre Séguier. It met at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a> from 1672 to 1805, and since 1805 at the Palais de l'Institut.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The forty seats are numbered, and candidates apply for a particular seat. A new member is elected by the Académie itself, and must deliver a speech that includes a eulogy of the predecessor. Members are called les immortels after the motto À l'immortalité on Richelieu's seal. They hold office for life but can be dismissed for grave misconduct; the first dismissal, in 1638, was for theft, and 20 members have been expelled in all, four of them at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> for association with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> regime.</p><p>Marguerite Yourcenar was the first woman elected, in 1980. The Académie publishes the Dictionnaire de l'Académie française, whose first edition appeared in 1694, and awards more than sixty prizes, almost all created in the twentieth century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Académie Française” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the seventeenth century Descartes, Corneille, Molière and Racine, the <b>Académie française</b> founded in 1635 and the court of Versailles made French taste the standard of European elites, and French increasingly replaced Latin as the language of diplomacy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
